# Bash Conventions

## Safety header

Executable scripts (anything with a `#!/bin/bash` shebang and meant to be run directly) MUST start with:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail
```

<!-- Library files in scripts/lib/ are *sourced*, not executed. They intentionally OMIT
     `set -euo pipefail` so they inherit the caller's shell options without surprising
     side effects. -->

## Banner block

Every script and library starts with a header banner: a `# ====...` separator line, the title, a short purpose paragraph, requirements, and a `Usage:` block. Cite an exemplar script in this repo (e.g., ${CITATION_BASH}) and match its style.

## Naming

- Functions: `snake_case` with a module prefix tied to the file/library — e.g., `registry_init`, `registry_add`, `platform_detect`.
- Module prefix prevents collisions when multiple libs are sourced into the same script.

## Logging

Use the project's standard inline log markers (do not invent new ones):

- `[INFO]` — progress / informational
- `[OK]` — success
- `[WARN]` — non-fatal problem
- `[FAIL]` — fatal error before exiting non-zero

## Tool checks and quoting

- Check tool availability before invoking: `command -v jq >/dev/null || { echo "[FAIL] jq required"; exit 1; }`.
- Always quote variable expansions: `"$var"`, `"${arr[@]}"` — never bare `$var`. Word-splitting bugs are not worth the brevity.
- Prefer `[[ ... ]]` over `[ ... ]` for tests.
